<section id="about-tool" class="border-t border-hairline">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-24 sm:px-6 md:py-32">
    <p class="eyebrow">About the tool</p>
    <h2 class="heading-lg mt-3">More than a letter shuffler</h2>

    <div class="mt-8 space-y-5 text-base leading-7 text-body">
      <p>
        Type any word, name or phrase and the tool searches a dictionary of more than 274,000 English words for every combination that uses each of your letters exactly once. It is built for puzzles, name ideas and plain curiosity, and it runs entirely in your browser.
      </p>

      <h3 class="heading-md pt-4">What is an anagram?</h3>
      <p>
        An anagram is a word or phrase made by rearranging the letters of another, using every letter once. “Listen” becomes “silent”, “evil” becomes “vile”, and “dormitory” becomes “dirty room”. The ones people remember are those where the new phrase still makes sense, and sometimes comments on the original. The habit is centuries old, from poets who hid messages in names to modern crossword setters.
      </p>

      <h3 class="heading-md pt-4">Multi-word results you can steer</h3>
      <p>
        Many tools stop at single words. Here a short name can become several phrases, and you decide how they look. Set the fewest and most words per result, require a word with Must Include, ban words with Never Include, and choose between everyday vocabulary and the full dictionary. Results are sorted with the shortest first, so readable phrases rise to the top. Good uses include usernames, band names, pen names, slogans and clues.
      </p>

      <h3 class="heading-md pt-4">Three tools, three jobs</h3>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-3 pl-6">
        <li>
          The <a class="text-link underline-offset-2 hover:underline" href="/anagram-solver/">anagram solver</a> uses all your letters to build phrases. It suits names, titles and cryptic crossword clues.
        </li>
        <li>
          The <a class="text-link underline-offset-2 hover:underline" href="/word-finder/">word finder</a> lists single words you can spell from some of your letters, with start and end filters. It suits Scrabble racks and crossword patterns.
        </li>
        <li>
          The <a class="text-link underline-offset-2 hover:underline" href="/anagram-checker/">anagram checker</a> compares two phrases and tells you whether they match.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h3 class="heading-md pt-4">Who it is for</h3>
      <p>
        Teachers use anagrams to warm up vocabulary lessons. Writers hide clues in mystery plots. Puzzle fans check their answers, parents make games for children, and many people simply type their own name to see what comes out.
      </p>

      <h3 class="heading-md pt-4">How to use it</h3>
      <ol class="list-decimal space-y-2 pl-6">
        <li>Enter a word, a name or a sentence. Spaces, punctuation and capitals are ignored.</li>
        <li>Choose a mode: full anagrams, single words, or a two-phrase check.</li>
        <li>Adjust the options if you want fewer, longer or more specific results.</li>
        <li>Copy a favorite, star it, download the list as TXT or CSV, or share a link that reopens the exact search.</li>
      </ol>

      <h3 class="heading-md pt-4">Private by design</h3>
      <p>
        The dictionary downloads once and every search runs in a background thread in your browser. Nothing you type is uploaded, and your history and favorites stay on your own device. There is no sign-up and no limit on searches.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
